      "text": "We start with a right cone that has a base circumference of \\(16\\pi\\) inches and a height of 30 inches. The volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nwhere \\(r\\) is the radius of the base and \\(h\\) is the height. Given the circumference \\(C\\) of the base is \\(16\\pi\\), we can find the radius \\(r\\) by using the formula for the circumference of a circle, \\(C = 2\\pi r\\):\n\\[\n16\\pi = 2\\pi r \\implies r = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nNow, substituting \\(r = 8\\) and \\(h = 30\\) into the volume formula, we get:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30 = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 1920 = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\nNext, we need to find the height of the shorter cone with a volume of \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches. Using the volume formula again, we set up the equation:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n192 = \\frac{1}{3} r^2 h \\implies 576 = r^2 h \\implies h = \\frac{576}{r^2}\n\\]\nWe know the circumferences are the same, so the radius \\(r\\) must be the same for both cones. Therefore, the height \\(h\\) of the shorter cone is:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{576}{8^2} = \\frac{576}{64} = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\frac{h}{30} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\n\\]",
